 the $y$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve the resulting equation for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x+4y & =5 \\\\ {0}^{2}+{y}^{2}-0+4y & =5 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+4y+4 & =5+4 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ y & =-2\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$-intercepts occur at the points $(0,-5)$ and $(0,1),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13959190/assets/1e4f7c975282aee647b2fa161ba34abac5c19e6bc3fc68f812533a0eb527834a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-8795/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6f0c89e-fe19-5aaf-9f85-b5f0ff7a344e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-8795"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-15692/selection/30cd9261e7a467e5dec42292136728b8ab63c97e4890ec934df4e75bc6408acb" :answer/id #uuid "b7767f20-469a-5b49-9722-5ca0d4953b10" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,6-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,6+\\sqrt{3})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15692/selection/18c6cf32474bb7bf55050b5c6b85e7842e1df97b178fc4de605e0f6271f6c3ce" :answer/id #uuid "365c2cc9-c09c-5186-a5b2-cbc1b37e4c50" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,4-\\sqrt{3})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{3})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15692/selection/e60092100145df2413239cfba8e47d88b6bbc80f7968dd2ae6c0fbde3f1c2b48" :answer/id #uuid "fa03cb03-a289-5f78-ab13-b8ebd0c4b8c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,4-\\sqrt{5})$ and $(0,4+\\sqrt{5})$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15692/selection/46851c4b90b4e6f43054f45bdfd788477c972bdf9996432b2d2b48aba1d13995" :answer/id #uuid "2d992dcf-09b9-53f5-8bca-d6c180748bcb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(0,5-\\sqrt{3})